  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nBroad west-northwesterly flow is present across the region this \nafternoon, to the south of a deep upper low up over the Hudson Bay. \nAt the surface, high pressure is centered over the Missouri River \nValley, keeping quiet conditions in place over eastern KS. Even with \nmostly sunny skies, high temperatures are remaining in the 80s, a \nwelcome change from most of last week. The surface high will depart \neast tonight, allowing southerly winds to return in response to a \ndeepening low over the High Plains. Farther to our northwest, \nexpecting storms to develop this evening along the surface trough \nacross portions of South Dakota and Nebraska. These storms will move \nsoutheast into eastern Nebraska by tomorrow morning.\n\nHow these storms evolve overnight will play a large role in our \nthunderstorm potential Tuesday. Some low potential for gusty winds \ncould exist with this first round, but the more interesting \npotential will be with potential evening storms in their wake. While \nshear will be plentiful, increasing to around 40 kts by evening, \nthermodynamics will be dependent on the extent of the morning \nstorms. The more organized and farther west morning storms are, the \nfarther west outflow will extend. This would limit the potential for \nsurface-based storms to redevelop, or at least confine the potential \nto central KS. On the other hand, if outflow remains weak and better \nheating (temps rising into the 90s) can develop across the area, \nthere would be a window for surface based supercells with large hail \nand damaging winds. The former scenario seems a bit more likely at \nthis time, but will need to consistently monitor the mesoscale setup \nover the next 24 hours. There is better confidence in slightly more \nelevated storms develop overnight as the main cold front shifts \nsouth. These storms would not have as high of a figurative ceiling \nas any earlier surface-based supercells, but could nonetheless \nproduce some large hail and/or damaging winds.\n\nCooler temperatures will move in behind the front for Wednesday and \nThursday. Highs should stay in the 80s, perhaps even 70s on \nWednesday if cloud cover can remain persistent. Some lingering \nmoisture will remain in place behind the cold front, so it's not out \nof the question we see a few additional thunderstorms Wednesday PM. \nBut the main large scale forcing should stay to our north and west, \nso think things will generally stay dry. Uncertainty increases by \nthe weekend as the upper flow amplifies. Does seem like we should \ntrend a bit warmer as ridging builds to our west, but with northwest \nflow overhead we should stay fairly close to average. Precipitation \nchances will remain tied to low-predictability shortwaves moving \nthrough the northwest flow aloft. Slightly better signal for one of \nthese to pass nearby Friday into Saturday, but confidence is low \nthis far out.",
